From bab48e557c262085a7ca6f23e020a59546b9d585 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:22:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] eip155: answer a retried pending settlement from its
 transaction
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

x402 v2 §9 makes settlement_pending non-terminal: the caller reconciles and
may send the same settle request again. The TS SDK's resource server does so
once, immediately (settleWithPendingRetry). x402-rs kept no record of the
first attempt, so the retry was settled from scratch: a second broadcast
while the first transaction was still pending, or invalid_transaction_state
once it had spent the nonce, although the payment had gone through.

- The v2 exact handler remembers, per settle request (the hash of the parsed
  request: payload, signature and requirements), the transaction of every
  settle that ended in settlement_pending. The record is in memory, bounded
  (10,000 entries, oldest dropped first) and expires after 15 minutes.
- A retry of the same request sends nothing. It looks up the receipt and answers
  success with that transaction once it is mined, settlement_pending with the
  same hash while it is not, and invalid_transaction_state with the hash if it
  reverted.
- A settlement transaction that was mined but reverted is now reported with its
  hash in transaction (new X402SchemeFacilitatorError::TransactionFailed, x402
  v2 §9 invalid_transaction_state, HTTP 400) instead of unexpected_error with
  "transaction": "": §5.3.2 allows "" only if nothing was broadcast. Such a
  revert is no longer re-explained as a replay.

Only identical requests share a record, so a different payload can never be
answered with another payment's transaction.
